Hello team,
I have been writing a ranked function for NIMBLE to implement posterior predictive checks, the one from Bugs works in the following way:
ranked(x,s) brings the s smallest component of v.

The idea is to sort the values of x and bring the s value that is of interest.

The sort function is:
Sort.Func<-nimbleFunction(
  run=function(X=double(1)){
    X<-X[!is.na <http://is.na>(X)]
    N<-length(X)
    if(N>1){
    for(M in 1:(N-1)){
      for(I in 1:(N-M)){
        if(X[I]>X[I+1]){
          temp<-X[I]
          X[I]<-X[I+1]
          X[I+1]<-temp
        }
      }
    }
    }
  return(X)} )

In the code is just used once as is show below:

code <- nimbleCode({
  for(i in 1:facility) {
    b.Facility[i]~dnorm(mu.F, tau.F)
    sort.b.Fac[i]<-Sort.Func(b.Facility[1:facility])[i]
    ..... #some extra code ....

Once I load the model via nimbleMode I get the following warning:
     Warning message:
     In code[[2]] == names(dimensionsList):
       longer object length is not a multiple of shorter object length
Once I compile the model I get the following error:
     Error in sizeIndexingBracket(<environment>, new("symbolTable", .xData = <environment>),  :
       Error in 'rcFun_R_GlobalEnv21(X=Interm_129)[getNodeFunctionIndexedInfo(ARG1_INDEXEDNODEINFO__,1)]';
       has 'rcFun_R_GlobalEnv21(X=Interm_129)' been created?

I guess that during the comparison of the sorting function I am comparing 2 different sizes of vector and therefore is bringing the warning message.
Is there something that I am missing in the code or the nimble function that it does not make it work??

Thank you in advance for your time :)

Hi all,

Sorry, I accidentally posted this without finishing it earlier!

I have a problem in that I can't get nested nimbleFunctions to compile when using NAs as default values.
As a reprex, I've used the custom distribution example from the NIMBLE manual below.

The pmyexp function takes an optional truncation argument (zU below) which by default is a missing value.
This compiles fine. However, if I then call this function within another nimbleFunction (rmyexp below)
it won't compile. I think I've narrowed it down to the fact that the pmyexp(zU, rate) doesn't explicitly set zU, but I have
no idea how to do this. It should reduce to the default I think. I've tried various things, including setting dummy
variables within rmyexp, but cannot seem to get this to work.

Any pointers greatly appreciated.

Many thanks,

TJ

library(nimble)

## set up dummy CDF
pmyexp <- nimbleFunction(
  run = function(q = double(0), rate = double(0, default = 1),
                 zU = double(0, default = as.numeric(NA)),
                 lower.tail = integer(0, default = 1),
                 log.p = integer(0, default = 0)) {
    returnType(double(0))
    if(!is.na <http://is.na>(zU)) {
      ldenom <- log(1.0 - exp(-rate * zU))
    } else {
      ldenom <- 0
    }
    if(!lower.tail) {
      logp <- -rate * q - ldenom
      if(log.p) return(logp) else return(exp(logp))
    } else {
      p <- log(1 - exp(-rate * q)) - ldenom
      if(!log.p) return(exp(p))
      else return(p)
    }
  })

## set up dummy random sampler
rmyexp <- nimbleFunction(
  run = function(n = integer(0), rate = double(0, default = 1),
                 zU = double(0, default = as.numeric(NA))) {
    returnType(double(0))
    if(n != 1) print("rmyexp only allows n = 1; using n = 1.")
    dev <- runif(1, 0, 1)
    if(!is.na <http://is.na>(zU)) {
        dev <- dev * pmyexp(zU, rate)
    }
    return(-log(1-dev) / rate)
})

## compiles fine
compileNimble(pmyexp)

## try to compile and throws an error
## (should use default zU = NA in pmyexp call below)
compileNimble(rmyexp)

